                  "kind": "function"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-193",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "CWE-193 - Off-by-one Error\nVulnerability: Heap Buffer Overflow via Off-by-One Error in buffer_write\nDescription: A heap-based buffer overflow vulnerability exists in the buffer_write function\ndue to an off-by-one error in the bounds checking logic. The function uses\n'index > buf->size' instead of 'index >= buf->size' to validate array access,\nallowing an attacker to write one byte past the end of the allocated buffer.\n\nFor a buffer of size N (valid indices 0 to N-1), the incorrect check allows\nindex == N to pass through, resulting in an out-of-bounds write at buf->buf[N].\nThis can corrupt heap metadata or adjacent heap objects, potentially leading to\narbitrary code execution or denial of service.\n\nThe vulnerability is triggered through user-controlled input that satisfies\nspecific arithmetic constraints and provides an index value equal to the buffer\nsize (300 in the POV). After 1000 levels of deep recursion, the buffer_write\nfunction is called with index=300 on a buffer of size 300, causing a heap\nbuffer overflow detected by AddressSanitizer.\n"
          },
